Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [configuration specific]

usb: core: disable USB3 U1/U2 LPM system-wide

On EDGEPLANT T1 the USB3.0 links behind the internal TUSB8041 hub fail to
recover from the U1/U2 low-power link states while idle, which triggers a
disconnect and re-enumeration of the whole USB3.0 bus. The hub IC register
(u1u2Disable) cannot be set because the SMBus is not wired on the T1 board,
so U1/U2 has to be disabled on the host (kernel) side.

usb_device_supports_lpm() is the single gate that decides whether the kernel
programs U1/U2 timeouts and allows device-initiated LPM. Reporting no LPM for
any SuperSpeed-or-faster device disables U1/U2 regardless of the device
VID:PID or host controller. USB 2.0 (High-Speed and below) LPM (L1) and
runtime autosuspend (U3) are not affected.

The failure has only been observed on the T1, but U1/U2 is a host- and
device-independent USB3 mechanism, so the patch is applied to all supported
devices as a precaution.

The hunk is anchored at the function signature so the single patch applies
unchanged to every target kernel tree (4.9 through 6.18).
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/core/hub.c b/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
index 2bd0eb7a6e3a..b28d4a82cb3d 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
@@ -132,4 +132,12 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(usb_hub_to_struct_hub);
 int usb_device_supports_lpm(struct usb_device *udev)
 {
+	/* Disable USB3 (SuperSpeed+) U1/U2 LPM system-wide: a failed U1/U2
+	 * exit disconnects the whole USB3 bus. Observed on EDGEPLANT T1
+	 * (TUSB8041 hub; SMBus is unwired, so the hub-side u1u2Disable is
+	 * unreachable). Applied on all boards as a precaution because U1/U2
+	 * is host-controller and device independent. */
+	if (udev->speed >= USB_SPEED_SUPER)
+		return 0;
+
 	/* Some devices have trouble with LPM */
 	if (udev->quirks & USB_QUIRK_NO_LPM)
